WEBVTT

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.110
Now I’m going to rehearse my presentation

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.190
How do I look?

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.240 
Do I look stiff?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.190
I’m so nervous

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.130
I can tell you are nervous from the way you’re standing

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.160
Body language plays an important role in a presentation

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.010 
It’s a form of non-verbal communication

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.040
I know that I can’t slouch or lean back against the wall

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.000
What else can I do to keep good posture?

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.290
Keep your back straight

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.030 
and put your shoulders back

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.110 
That will make you look more confident

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.010
Also, breathe smoothly

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.150
Where should I put my hands? 

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.020
I shouldn’t put them in my pockets, right?

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.260
Speaking with your hands

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:46.200 
will help keep the audience’s attention

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.130 
When you use an open palm gesture

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.260
it shows that you are honest and sincere   

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.010
Gestures are not only hand movements though, right? 

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.090
They include movements of the head

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.220 
shoulders and other parts of the body

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.290 
Yes. Nodding is a good way to express your friendliness

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.240 
Especially during the Q&A period

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.000  
this positive gesture will encourage questions from the audience

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.040 
Should I stand still behind the podium or walk around?

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.000 
I tend to stare at the slides or speak to my notes when I get nervous

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.020 
You can move a little but not all over the place

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.210  
Remember to make eye contact

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.120  
When you look people in the eye

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.020  
it shows you are interested in them and have respect for them

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.240 
Sometimes when an audience loses interest or isn’t paying attention

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.240 
they sit back, look away or have their eyes closed

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.180 
That’s the feedback you get from their body language

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.180 
So, carefully watch for their responses

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.000  
and quickly adjust what you’re saying 

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.020 
or doing to get their attention back

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.220 
Thanks for the tips

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.200  
I have to practice in front of a mirror 

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.220 
so that I’ll feel comfortable during the presentation.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.160 
That’s great

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.270 
Last but not least, practice your facial expressions

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.160 
You should always smile

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.100  
Remember: smiling is the most powerful weapon.

